1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,720
On the thirtieth of June 2012, the world
added one second to the clock.

2
00:00:06,067 --> 00:00:08,251
Within minutes, Reddit went down.

3
00:00:08,301 --> 00:00:12,109
An airline in Australia couldn't
check in its passengers.

4
00:00:12,159 --> 00:00:14,411
Servers across the internet froze.

5
00:00:14,733 --> 00:00:20,289
All because of a second that, on most
clocks, isn't even allowed to exist.

6
00:00:20,633 --> 00:00:24,945
That second exists because of something most
people never hear: right now,

7
00:00:24,995 --> 00:00:27,657
nobody on Earth knows exactly
what time it is.

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:30,283
Not your phone. Not the
satellites overhead.

9
00:00:30,333 --> 00:00:33,888
The official time for this moment will be
worked out weeks from now,

10
00:00:33,938 --> 00:00:35,319
in an office outside Paris.

11
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:40,779
This is the story of who decides what time it is
— and why we stopped trusting the

12
00:00:40,829 --> 00:00:41,729
Earth to do it.

13
00:00:42,067 --> 00:00:44,607
For most of history, time
came from the Sun.

14
00:00:44,657 --> 00:00:47,197
When it was highest in the
sky, it was noon.

15
00:00:47,533 --> 00:00:49,834
Which meant every town had its own noon.

16
00:00:49,884 --> 00:00:52,949
In Bristol, it came about ten
minutes after London's.

17
00:00:53,266 --> 00:00:58,419
The clock on Bristol's Corn Exchange still has
two minute hands. One for Bristol.

18
00:00:58,469 --> 00:00:59,382
One for London.

19
00:00:59,699 --> 00:01:00,998
Then came the railways.

20
00:01:01,048 --> 00:01:05,632
A timetable with a different time at every
station was a recipe for collisions.

21
00:01:05,682 --> 00:01:08,271
In 1880, Britain made
Greenwich time the law.

22
00:01:08,599 --> 00:01:11,930
And a second was still just a
slice of a day: one

23
00:01:11,980 --> 00:01:15,931
eighty-six-thousand-four-hundredth
of a turn of the Earth.

24
00:01:16,266 --> 00:01:19,979
There was only one problem. The
Earth is a terrible clock.

25
00:01:20,299 --> 00:01:22,181
The tides drag on it like a brake.

26
00:01:22,231 --> 00:01:26,498
Earthquakes, melting ice and the churning
core nudge it faster, then slower.

27
00:01:26,548 --> 00:01:28,771
No two days are exactly the same length.

28
00:01:29,099 --> 00:01:34,331
So in the 1950s, scientists stopped looking up
at the sky — and looked inside an

29
00:01:34,381 --> 00:01:35,189
atom instead.

30
00:01:35,533 --> 00:01:38,903
A caesium atom absorbs microwaves
at one exact frequency.

31
00:01:38,953 --> 00:01:43,223
Every caesium atom in the universe has the
same one. It never wears out.

32
00:01:43,273 --> 00:01:44,423
It never slows down.

33
00:01:44,766 --> 00:01:49,641
In 1955, near London, Louis Essen built the
first clock that counted those

34
00:01:49,691 --> 00:01:53,834
microwaves. And it showed something
awkward almost immediately.

35
00:01:54,166 --> 00:01:58,488
The atom was keeping perfect time. It was
the planet that was wobbling.

36
00:01:58,833 --> 00:02:03,274
Astronomers had kept the world's time for
centuries. They did not give it up easily.

37
00:02:03,324 --> 00:02:04,397
It took twelve years.

38
00:02:04,733 --> 00:02:10,198
In 1967, the second was redefined: nine billion,
one hundred and ninety-two million,

39
00:02:10,248 --> 00:02:12,561
six hundred and thirty-one thousand,

40
00:02:12,611 --> 00:02:16,303
seven hundred and seventy
vibrations of caesium. Exactly.

41
00:02:16,633 --> 00:02:20,492
That solved one problem and created
another. Atomic time is perfect.

42
00:02:20,542 --> 00:02:24,919
Earth time is not. And people still want noon
to happen when the Sun is high.

43
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:33,354
So since 1972, whenever the planet falls
almost a second behind the atoms,

44
00:02:33,404 --> 00:02:39,868
the world adds one. 23:59:59. Then
23:59:60. Then midnight.

45
00:02:40,199 --> 00:02:42,548
It has happened twenty-seven times.

46
00:02:42,598 --> 00:02:46,181
And the one in 2012 is the one
that took down Reddit.

47
00:02:46,499 --> 00:02:49,302
Software assumes a minute has
sixty seconds.

48
00:02:49,352 --> 00:02:52,349
Give it sixty-one, and some of
it simply stops.

49
00:02:52,666 --> 00:02:54,738
Google stopped playing along.

50
00:02:54,788 --> 00:02:58,396
Now it smears the extra second
across a whole day,

51
00:02:58,446 --> 00:03:04,176
running its clocks very slightly slow until
the second has quietly disappeared.

52
00:03:04,499 --> 00:03:06,438
In 2022, the world gave up.

53
00:03:06,488 --> 00:03:09,532
The leap second will be abolished by 2035,

54
00:03:09,582 --> 00:03:13,879
and atomic time and the Sun will be
allowed to drift apart.

55
00:03:14,199 --> 00:03:18,377
But there's a twist. Lately, the Earth
has been spinning faster.

56
00:03:18,427 --> 00:03:23,662
For the first time in history, the next leap
second might have to be taken away.

57
00:03:23,999 --> 00:03:27,509
So who actually decides what time
it is? Not one clock.

58
00:03:27,559 --> 00:03:30,487
Even atomic clocks drift, each
in its own way.

59
00:03:30,833 --> 00:03:35,862
Instead, more than four hundred atomic clocks
in about eighty laboratories send

60
00:03:35,912 --> 00:03:40,169
their readings to the International
Bureau of Weights and Measures,

61
00:03:40,219 --> 00:03:41,391
just outside Paris.

62
00:03:41,733 --> 00:03:46,842
There they are compared and averaged. The
steadiest clocks get the biggest vote.

63
00:03:46,892 --> 00:03:51,743
The result is Coordinated Universal Time —
the time the whole world runs on.

64
00:03:52,066 --> 00:03:53,757
And here is the strange part.

65
00:03:53,807 --> 00:03:58,259
The answer is published once a month — for
the month that has already gone.

66
00:03:58,599 --> 00:04:01,062
Official time only ever
exists in the past.

67
00:04:01,112 --> 00:04:05,971
Every clock you own is showing a prediction of
what that answer will turn out to be.

68
00:04:06,021 --> 00:04:09,069
Usually right to within a few
billionths of a second.

69
00:04:09,399 --> 00:04:13,559
Your phone gets its time from satellites,
twenty thousand kilometres up,

70
00:04:13,609 --> 00:04:15,196
each carrying atomic clocks.

71
00:04:15,533 --> 00:04:19,885
Up there, time runs differently. Weaker
gravity makes their clocks run fast.

72
00:04:19,935 --> 00:04:21,739
Their speed makes them run slow.

73
00:04:21,789 --> 00:04:26,141
Add it up, and each one gains thirty-eight
millionths of a second every day.

74
00:04:26,466 --> 00:04:31,762
Leave that uncorrected, and your map would be
wrong by around ten kilometres — by

75
00:04:31,812 --> 00:04:32,356
tomorrow.

76
00:04:32,699 --> 00:04:36,738
So the satellite clocks are built to tick
slightly slow on the ground.

77
00:04:36,788 --> 00:04:38,841
In orbit, Einstein makes them right.

78
00:04:39,166 --> 00:04:42,171
The newest clocks use light
instead of microwaves.

79
00:04:42,221 --> 00:04:46,082
Lift one by a single centimetre, and it
measurably ticks faster.

80
00:04:46,399 --> 00:04:48,744
Time isn't even the same from
floor to floor.

81
00:04:48,794 --> 00:04:52,949
Walk upstairs, and your head ages faster than
your feet — we made a whole video

82
00:04:52,999 --> 00:04:53,535
about that.

83
00:04:53,866 --> 00:04:59,337
So what time is it, right now? The honest
answer is: we'll know next month.

84
00:04:59,387 --> 00:05:02,502
Until then, four hundred
clocks are voting.

85
00:05:02,833 --> 00:05:06,269
The last digit of time is always a
little bit in the future.
